      "sol": "<div class=\"expanded-practice-solution\"><b>Answer D.</b><p>Histogram heights are frequency densities, not frequencies. The area of each bar supplies the number of observations, and those frequencies weight the class midpoints in the estimated mean.</p><p>Multiply density by class width to recover frequency. The first width is 10, giving frequency \\(2\\times10=20\\). The middle width is 20, giving \\(20k\\). The last width is 20, giving \\((3/2)20=30\\). The total frequency is \\(50+20k\\).</p><p>The class midpoints are 5,20,40. The estimated total of all measurements is \\(20(5)+(20k)(20)+30(40)=100+400k+1200=1300+400k\\).</p><p>The estimated mean is therefore \\((1300+400k)/(50+20k)=24\\). Multiply through: \\(1300+400k=1200+480k\\). Thus \\(100=80k\\), giving \\(k=5/4\\).</p><p>The middle frequency is \\(20(5/4)=25\\), so the total is \\(20+25+30=75\\). Check: the midpoint total is \\(100+500+1200=1800\\), and \\(1800/75=24\\).</p><p>An alternative calculation uses deviations from the given mean \\(24\\). The total weighted deviation must be zero:\n\\[20(5-24)+(20k)(20-24)+30(40-24)=0.\\]\nThis simplifies to \\(-380-80k+480=0\\), hence \\(k=5/4\\). It gives the same total of \\(75\\), while making clear that the wider classes carry weights proportional to their widths.</p></div>"
