      "sol": "<div class=\"expanded-practice-solution\"><b>Answer A.</b><p>Substituting the line into the circle produces a quadratic whose real roots correspond to intersections. The word “distinct” requires a strictly positive discriminant, excluding tangencies.</p><p>Substitute y=mx into the circle equation: \\((1+m^2)x^2-(6+4m)x+9=0\\). Its leading coefficient is always positive, so it is a genuine quadratic for every real m. Each distinct real x gives one distinct point on the line.</p><p>Two distinct real roots require positive discriminant. Here it is \\((6+4m)^2-36(1+m^2)=36+48m+16m^2-36-36m^2=48m-20m^2\\).</p><p>Thus \\(4m(12-5m)>0\\). The zeros are 0 and 12/5, and the product is positive precisely between them. The answer is \\(0<m<12/5\\). At either endpoint the discriminant is zero and the line is tangent, so those endpoints are excluded.</p><p>For the sign condition, if \\(m<0\\) then \\(m\\) is negative and \\(12-5m\\) is positive, so their product is negative. If \\(0<m<12/5\\), both factors are positive. If \\(m>12/5\\), the second is negative. These three cases justify the complete interval, not just a plausible range inferred from the two roots.</p></div>"
