      "sol": "<div class=\"expanded-practice-solution\"><b>Answer D.</b><p>Squaring each term of a series is different from squaring its sum. The squared terms form a new geometric series with their own first term and ratio.</p><p>Let the original first term be a and ratio r, with \\(|r|<1\\). The first sum gives \\(a/(1-r)=9\\), hence \\(a=9(1-r)\\). The squared terms have first term \\(a^2\\) and ratio \\(r^2\\), so their sum is \\(a^2/(1-r^2)=81/2\\).</p><p>Substitute a and factor the denominator: \\(81(1-r)^2/[(1-r)(1+r)]=81/2\\). Since \\(|r|<1\\), neither 1-r nor 1+r is zero, so cancellation gives \\((1-r)/(1+r)=1/2\\).</p><p>Cross-multiplication gives \\(2-2r=1+r\\), so \\(3r=1\\) and r=1/3. Then \\(a=9(1-1/3)=9(2/3)=6\\). Check the squared sum: \\(36/(1-1/9)=36(9/8)=81/2\\).</p><p>Explicitly, the original terms are \\(a,ar,ar^2,\\ldots\\), while the squared terms are\n\\[a^2,a^2r^2,a^2r^4,\\ldots.\\]\nTheir common ratio is \\(r^2\\), giving denominator \\(1-r^2\\) in the sum formula. Squaring the original sum would instead introduce cross terms, so \\(81/2\\) cannot be set equal to \\((a/(1-r))^2\\).</p></div>"
