      "sol": "<div class=\"expanded-practice-solution\"><b>Answer D.</b><p>The given point is a point on each tangent, not the point of contact with the curve. Introduce an unknown contact coordinate \\(t\\), then impose the condition that its tangent passes through \\((2,4)\\).</p><p>At a contact point \\((t,t^3)\\), the gradient is \\(3t^2\\). The tangent equation is \\(y-t^3=3t^2(x-t)\\), or \\(y=3t^2x-2t^3\\).</p><p>Passing through (2,4) requires \\(4=6t^2-2t^3\\). Divide by -2 and rearrange: \\(t^3-3t^2+2=0\\). Substitution shows t=1 is a root; division by t-1 gives \\((t-1)(t^2-2t-2)=0\\).</p><p>The quadratic roots are \\([2\\pm\\sqrt{4+8}]/2=1\\pm\\sqrt3\\). Together with t=1, these are three distinct real contact points. They give distinct tangent lines: equality of both the coefficients \\(3t^2\\) and \\(-2t^3\\) would force the contact inputs equal, since the real cube is one-to-one.</p><p>Therefore there are exactly three distinct tangents. The fact that (2,4) is not itself on the curve does not prevent multiple tangents passing through it.</p><p>The factorisation can be checked explicitly:\n\\[(t-1)(t^2-2t-2)=t^3-3t^2+2.\\]\nEach resulting contact input gives exactly one tangent because the cubic is differentiable everywhere. Counting contact points alone would require care if different points could have the same tangent, so the comparison of both gradient and intercept above is necessary to complete the count.</p></div>"
