      "n": 15,
      "correct": 2,
      "sol": "<div class=\"expanded-practice-solution\"><b>Answer C.</b><p>The fixed garden area allows one dimension to be eliminated. Pay attention to which total increase belongs to each dimension before expanding the outer area.</p><p>Let x be the garden dimension that increases by 2. Since its area is 36, its other dimension is 36/x, with x>0. The outer rectangle has dimensions x+2 and 36/x+4.</p><p>Its area is \\(A(x)=(x+2)(36/x+4)=36+4x+72/x+8=44+4x+72/x\\). Differentiate the powers: \\(A'(x)=4-72/x^2\\). The stationary equation gives \\(4x^2=72\\), so \\(x^2=18\\) and \\(x=3\\sqrt2\\).</p><p>For \\(0<x<3\\sqrt2\\), the derivative is negative; for x greater than that it is positive. Hence this is the minimum over all positive dimensions. The other garden dimension is \\(36/(3\\sqrt2)=6\\sqrt2\\), also positive.</p><p>Substitute into the area: \\(44+4(3\\sqrt2)+72/(3\\sqrt2)=44+12\\sqrt2+12\\sqrt2=44+24\\sqrt2\\). The paths add to dimensions, not directly to the garden area.</p><p>There is also an algebraic minimum proof using a square:\n\\[4x+\\frac{72}{x}-24\\sqrt2=\\left(2\\sqrt x-\\frac{6\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt x}\\right)^2\\ge0.\\]\nEquality requires \\(2x=6\\sqrt2\\), so \\(x=3\\sqrt2\\). Hence the outer area is at least \\(44+24\\sqrt2\\), with the same attainable dimensions as in the calculus solution.</p></div>"
