      "sol": "<div class=\"expanded-practice-solution\"><b>Answer B.</b><p>The second intersection can lie on either side of the origin. A geometric area is positive regardless of the order in which those intersection coordinates occur.</p><p>Equating the graphs gives \\(x^2-(m+2)x=0\\), so the intersections have x=0 and x=c, where c=m+2. Between these roots the line is above the parabola: the vertical difference is \\(cx-x^2=x(c-x)>0\\), whether c is positive or negative.</p><p>An antiderivative of the gap is \\(cx^2/2-x^3/3\\). The integral from 0 to c equals \\(c^3/2-c^3/3=c^3/6\\). If c is negative, those limits run right to left; the positive area is therefore the absolute value, \\(|c|^3/6\\).</p><p>Set \\(|c|^3/6=36\\), giving \\(|c|^3=216\\) and \\(|c|=6\\). Hence c=6 or c=-6. Since m=c-2, the two slopes are 4 and -8, whose sum is -4. The zero value c=0 would enclose no area and is automatically excluded.</p><p>For \\(c>0\\), integrate \\(cx-x^2\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(c\\). For \\(c<0\\), integrate the same positive gap from \\(c\\) to \\(0\\), giving\n\\[\\left[\\frac{cx^2}{2}-\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_{c}^{0}=-\\frac{c^3}{6}>0.\\]\nThus \\(|c|^3/6\\) correctly covers both configurations. Using only \\(c^3/6=36\\) would miss the negative-intersection case and one of the two slopes.</p></div>"
