      "sol": "<div class=\"expanded-practice-solution\"><b>Answer D.</b><p>Positive integer intercepts turn the geometric problem into a finite factorisation problem. Complete a product involving the two intercept variables, then enumerate its positive factor pairs.</p><p>The intercept equation is \\(x/p+y/q=1\\), with p,q>0. Substituting (2,3) gives \\(2/p+3/q=1\\). Each summand is positive and less than 1, so p>2 and q>3.</p><p>Multiply by pq: \\(2q+3p=pq\\). Rearrange and factor by adding 6: \\(pq-3p-2q+6=6\\), giving \\((p-2)(q-3)=6\\). Both factors are positive integers.</p><p>The ordered factor pairs of 6 are (1,6),(2,3),(3,2),(6,1). They give the intercept pairs \\((p,q)=(3,9),(4,6),(5,5),(8,4)\\). These are distinct lines, and each passes through the required point.</p><p>Each triangle has area pq/2, so the four areas are \\(27/2,12,25/2,16\\). Their sum is \\((27+25)/2+28=26+28=54\\). Negative factor pairs are not allowed because the intercepts are positive and exceed 2 and 3.</p><p>The rearrangement is motivated by grouping:\n\\[pq-3p-2q=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p(q-3)-2(q-3)=6.\\]\nThis yields \\((p-2)(q-3)=6\\). The ordered pairs must remain ordered because adding \\(2\\) to one factor and \\(3\\) to the other treats them differently. Interchanging a factor pair generally produces a different line and a different area.</p></div>"
