      "n": 18,
      "correct": 1,
      "sol": "<div class=\"expanded-practice-solution\"><b>Answer B.</b><p>The condition concerns positive \\(x\\), not merely real values of \\(2^x\\). Shifting the transformed variable by \\(1\\) makes the positivity threshold easy to express using root signs.</p><p>Put \\(u=2^x\\), so \\(4^x=u^2\\). The equation becomes \\(u^2-(k+3)u+4k=0\\). Positive x corresponds precisely to u>1, because powers of 2 increase and \\(2^0=1\\).</p><p>Shift the threshold to zero by putting v=u-1. Expand: \\((v+1)^2-(k+3)(v+1)+4k=0\\), which simplifies to \\(v^2-(k+1)v+(3k-2)=0\\). We require two distinct positive roots v.</p><p>A monic quadratic has two distinct positive roots exactly when its discriminant is positive and its root sum and product are both positive. The positive product ensures the real roots have the same sign; the positive sum then makes that sign positive. Here these conditions are \\((k+1)^2-4(3k-2)>0\\), \\(k+1>0\\), and \\(3k-2>0\\).</p><p>The discriminant simplifies to \\(k^2-10k+9=(k-1)(k-9)\\), positive for k<1 or k>9. The product condition gives k>2/3, which already guarantees the positive sum. Intersecting gives \\(2/3<k<1\\) or k>9.</p><p>At k=2/3 one root has v=0 and hence x=0, which is not positive. At k=1 or 9 roots coincide. All endpoints are therefore excluded.</p><p>For the transformed quadratic in \\(v\\), positive product alone would allow two negative roots; positive sum alone would allow opposite signs. Together, and with a positive discriminant, they force two distinct positive roots. Each then gives exactly one \\(x=\\log_2(v+1)>0\\), so there is a one-to-one correspondence and the root count is preserved.</p></div>"
