      "sol": "<div class=\"expanded-practice-solution\"><b>Answer E.</b><p>Both equations are symmetric in \\(x,y\\). Their sum and product therefore reduce the system to one quadratic for the sum, followed by another for the individual numbers.</p><p>Put s=x+y and p=xy. The second equation says p=s. Also \\(x^2+y^2=(x+y)^2-2xy=s^2-2p\\), so the first equation becomes \\(s^2-2s=25\\). Thus \\(s^2-2s-25=0\\), giving \\(s=1\\pm\\sqrt{26}\\).</p><p>For each fixed s, x and y must be roots of \\(t^2-st+s=0\\), because their sum and product both equal s. Its discriminant is \\(s^2-4s=s(s-4)\\), positive if s<0 or s>4.</p><p>For \\(s=1-\\sqrt{26}\\), s<0. For \\(s=1+\\sqrt{26}\\), s>4 because \\(\\sqrt{26}>3\\). Thus each s gives two distinct real roots, say r and t, and two ordered pairs (r,t) and (t,r).</p><p>The pairs from different s values cannot coincide, because their sums differ. Every pair constructed this way has xy=s and \\(x^2+y^2=s^2-2s=25\\), so all are valid. The total is \\(2+2=4\\).</p><p>For a permitted sum \\(s\\), the individual values are\n\\[x,y=\\frac{s\\pm\\sqrt{s^2-4s}}2.\\]\nThe positive discriminant proves they differ, so exchanging them gives a second ordered pair. When \\(s<0\\), their product is negative and their signs are opposite; when \\(s>4\\), both are positive. This also distinguishes the two groups of pairs geometrically and confirms that they cannot overlap.</p></div>"
