      "sol": "<div class=\"expanded-practice-solution\"><b>Answer A.</b><p>The identities allow unit shifts and reflection in zero. Combining those operations fixes integer and half-integer inputs, but it does not automatically determine every fractional input.</p><p>Putting x=0 in the second identity gives f(0)=0. Repeated forward or backward unit shifts then give f(m)=2m for every integer m. Also \\(f(1/2)=f(-1/2)+2=-f(1/2)+2\\), so f(1/2)=1. Shifting this value by integers yields f(m+1/2)=2m+1. Together the integer and half-integer cases prove I for every integer n.</p><p>To test what is not forced, take \\(f(x)=2x+\\tfrac12\\sin(2\\pi x)\\). The sine term is odd and unchanged by adding 1 to x, so this function satisfies both identities. But f(1/4)=1, not 1/2, disproving II.</p><p>For the same function, f(3/4)=3/2-1/2=1=f(1/4). Two distinct inputs therefore have the same output, disproving III. Only I must hold. The counterexample is continuous as well: the missing hypothesis is not simply continuity.</p><p>For a half-integer, reflection changes \\(1/2\\) to \\(-1/2\\), and a unit shift takes it back, producing an equation involving the same unknown value twice:\n\\[f(1/2)=-f(1/2)+2.\\]\nThe counterexample adds to \\(2x\\) an odd term of period \\(1\\). That extra term disappears at all half-integers but changes some quarter-integer values, demonstrating exactly where freedom remains.</p></div>"
